  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nHot and humid conditions continue this afternoon as high \ntemperatures have warmed to the 80s and 90s with dew points in the \n60s and 70s. This is resulting in heat indices up to upper 80s and \n90s with a few locations touching 100F. A shortwave trough continues \nto take on a negative tilt over eastern Montana/western North \nDakota. This will keep the majority of strong to severe storms in \nthis area. This wave will also be responsible for a cold front that \nremains on track to push into the forecast area late tonight. Latest \nhi-res guidance shows little to no rain developing along this \nboundary due to large capping in place ahead of the boundary. At the \nsame time, cloud cover will increase aloft. Despite the front \npushing through the area, low temperatures look to stay quite warm \novernight with lows only falling to the 60s and 70s. In fact, some \nlocations will come within 3 degrees of their record warm lows. With \ndew points remaining in the 60s overnight, tonight will continue the \nmuggy conditions we have experienced this weekend.\n\nMonday will be similar to the weekend though a touch cooler as the \nthermal ridge gets pushed off to the south and east thanks to the \npreviously mentioned front. That said, high temperatures still look \nto remain in the 80s to potentially reaching 90F in a few locations. \nThe previously mentioned cold front will fully push through the \nforecast area by Monday afternoon, resulting in most likely another \ndry and hot day. Dew points will lower to the 50s and 60s, keeping \nhumidity values lower than what we saw this weekend. If rain were to \ndevelop along the front, thunderstorms may be possible though this \nis unlikely (<= 10% chance) to happen. Low temperatures will remain \nwarm though a bit cooler than Sunday's lows, with temperatures \nbottoming out in the 60s.\n\nA shortwave trough will eject out from the Rockies and into the \nNorthern Plains on Tuesday. The mass response from this wave will \nreturn the previously mentioned front northwards, but this time as a \nwarm front. Latest guidance still varies on how far north this front \nwill return. A few of the hi-res models show the front returning \nwell into the forecast area. But the ensembles as well as the \nmachine learning (ML) guidance keeps the front just south of highway-\n20 down to around I-80. Tough to say how far north the front will \nreturn but the ensembles continue to show moderate to high \nprobabilities (50-80%) for rainfall amounts to exceed a tenth of an \ninch of rain across the area, signaling at least elevated showers \nand storms are on the table north of the front. Instability values \nwill also depend on where the front sets up. Thus, severe weather \nremains possible as supported by the latest ML guidance showing a 5-\n15% chance for strong to severe storms. \n\nChances (20-40%) for rain will remain in the forecast for the rest \nof the week and next weekend. The highest probabilities look to come \non Wednesday and Thursday as a more active pattern continues aloft. \nMedium range guidance is becoming more consistent with upper level \nridging building over the central CONUS towards the end of next week \nand next weekend. This would result in warmer temperatures, \npotentially back into the 90s, and most likely drier conditions. In \nfact, this pattern is shaping up to be an omega block pattern. \nPattern recognition and forecast experience suggests that this \npattern may retrograde west over the coming days. The ensembles are \nalso in favor of this as their trends have already been amplifying \nthe ridge as well as pushing the ridge axis further west as well. \nSurface features are more uncertain, so questions remain regarding \nsurface moisture and resulting humidity. Will be keeping an eye on \nhow this pattern evolves over the course of the week.",
